offlinecompiler: a cache that cannot be published must not abort the compiler.

`0016` stopped an unwritable cache being published. This is the failure one step
later: a cache that was written correctly and *cannot be renamed into place*.

On Requiem it happened on every launch after the first:

    libc++abi: terminating due to uncaught exception of type
    std::__1::__fs::filesystem::filesystem_error: filesystem error: in rename:
    Access denied.
    ["C:\vessel\fexcache\cache/re9.exe-a7b10b28eeac3098-….new"]
    ["C:\vessel\fexcache\cache/re9.exe-a7b10b28eeac3098-…"]

`std::filesystem::rename`'s throwing overload, never caught -- not here, not in
`GenerateCache`, not in `main`. So `std::terminate` aborted the child and the
parent printed only `Cache generation failed for {}`, discarding every other
diagnostic that child still had to give. The 339 MB it had just spent minutes
producing went with it.

WHY THE RENAME IS DENIED IS A RULE IN OUR OWN WINE, and naming it is what makes
the fix below correct rather than hopeful. `server/fd.c:set_fd_name` refuses to
replace a destination that any fd still has open:

    /* can't replace an opened file */
    if ((inode = get_inode( st.st_dev, st.st_ino, -1 )))
    {
        int is_empty = list_empty( &inode->open );
        …
        if (!is_empty) set_error( STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED );

It never consults share modes at all, and a *mapped view* keeps such an fd alive
long after every handle is closed -- `server/mapping.c` grabs the mapping's fd
for the lifetime of the view. `ImageTracker::LoadAOTImages` maps the previous
cache at image load and never unmaps it, so from that moment the name cannot be
replaced by anything inside the prefix. This is why `FILE_SHARE_DELETE` is not
the fix, and why closing handles earlier is not either: they are closed already.

So publication moves out of the guest instead of being retried inside it. The
finished cache is renamed to `<final>.ready` -- a name nothing ever opens or
maps, so that rename always succeeds -- and Vessel promotes it on the host
before the next session starts anything, where a plain Linux `rename(2)`
replaces a mapped file without complaint because wineserver is not involved.
See `fexCachePromote` in `SessionRuntime.kt`.

`.ready` rather than simply leaving `.new` behind, because the two states must be
told apart. `.new` is also what a compiler killed mid-write leaves, and promoting
one of those would publish a truncated cache. Only `.ready` is ever promoted, and
only the line after `SaveData` returned true can create one.

The unchecked `open` beside it is fixed in the same hunk because it produces the
same misleading error: on failure `fd` is -1, `SaveData`'s writes all fail and it
still returns true, and the rename is then attempted on a source that does not
exist -- so the message names the rename rather than the open that actually
failed. `O_TRUNC` is added for the neighbouring reason: without it a longer
leftover `.new` is shortened only by `SaveData`'s own `ftruncate`, which puts a
correctness guarantee in the wrong file.

**This does not make the cache complete.** `0017` still stops at the code buffer
ceiling, so re9.exe's cache covers roughly a quarter of its blocks, chosen in
address order rather than by how often they run. What changes is that the quarter
now survives to be loaded instead of being discarded and recompiled every launch.

diff --git a/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp b/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp
